WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:15.200
Our brief today for this panel is to explore
the ways in which artificial intelligence

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.690
can work for us.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:25.380
Augmented intelligence is the phrase, and
it's the benevolent face of artificial intelligence.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.590
But let's step back for a moment for a little
bit of history.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:37.000
John J.C. Licklider was a Harvard trained
psychologist at MIT teacher and he was the

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.590
first head of the technology office, at the
advanced research projects agency.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:49.160
He funded some of the basic research behind
the personal computer and what became the

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.160
Internet.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.850
And in 1960 he wrote a classic essay man,
computer symbiosis.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:01:04.580
And in it he said that the appropriate role
for computing was to augment same word, a

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.070
human knowledge and intelligence rather than
supplanted.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:13.720
And so for decades we've been debating this
issue back and forth, always in a new context.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.220
And it is, the theme seems familiar.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:23.580
The context today seems drastically different
and that's what our panel is going to explore

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:24.580
today.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:29.070
Our first guest is an IBM fellow and manager
of Multimedia Division at IBM Watson Research

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.070
Center.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:36.360
He reads IBM's research and development on
visual comprehension, including its use in

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.420
IBM Watson.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:43.690
Please welcome John R Smith.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:48.110
Next is the Arthur Zitrin professor of bioethics
and the director of the Center for bioethics

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.610
at New York University.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:55.630
He is also the author of current controversies
in bioethics and is the editor in chief for

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:57.850
the Journal of moral philosophy.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.850
Please welcome Matthew Liao.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:07.950
Our next panelist is vice president for products
and strategy for integrating AI, a Toronto

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:08.989
based startup.

00:02:08.989 --> 00:02:13.870
Her company uses artificial intelligence to
help businesses improve and transform customer

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:18.599
experiences to make those experiences more
natural, more human like.

00:02:18.599 --> 00:02:23.390
Please welcome Kathryn Hume

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:27.870
Also joining us is a professor at the NYU
Stern School of business and it's center for

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.420
data science.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:35.080
He is editor in chief of the journal big data
and he is also the founder of set capital

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.280
management tools.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.240
Machine learning software is used to make
automated trading decisions in the financial

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.250
markets.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.980
Please welcome Vasant Dar.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.550
So John, we'll start with you.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:53.550
Give us your take on artificial intelligence
and you what, what is different now and where

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.720
is it, where are we, where is it headed?

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.410
And give us a few examples from the work that
IBM is doing currently since you guys have

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:05.880
been doing this for decades to it.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.820
However it's been defined over the years.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:14.270
Sure, so I'm a research scientist at the IBM
and by training, you know, I'm an expert in

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:21.860
computer vision and at IBM, what this has
meant is I'm teaching Watson to see.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:29.390
But let me take a step back a little bit from
that, there was a seminal moment more recently,

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:38.160
in AI and in 2011 where IBM built a computer
system in a lot of ways right at the cusp

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:45.430
of this second push in AI that was able to
participate on Jeopardy.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:53.099
Not only was it able to compete against humans,
it was able to defeat the world champions

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:55.590
at Jeopardy.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:02.230
So this really opened the minds of a lot of
researchers and of course a lot of work has

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.159
continued since then.

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:10.099
And I think we're finding ourselves in a period
of AI where, again, it seems like amazing

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:12.770
things are possible.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:20.049
Since then, since 2011 of course, you know,
with Jeopardy all of the questions, we're

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:21.560
basically dealing with language.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.800
So there were no images on Jeopardy.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:31.600
However, since then the challenges around
computer vision where I, where I do work around

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:39.160
language translation, around speech recognition,
you know, many perceptual tasks, AI has been

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.440
able to make really great, great advances.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:48.370
And I think it's put us in a position now
where we can think of many different industries

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:56.300
and how we can take these capabilities, which
you know, are now doing amazing things and

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:01.580
combine them together with human expertise
to make really significant impact.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:06.150
Kathryn, you've written a bit about this,
but what, what do you think is the biggest

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.270
misperception about artificial intelligence?

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:12.449
Oh God, I think there's all sorts of misperceptions
that are out there.

00:05:12.449 --> 00:05:16.699
It makes headlines to talk about the end of
work and in and announced panel, we're talking

00:05:16.699 --> 00:05:21.360
about augmented intelligence versus the machine
suddenly replacing us all and we can all just

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.990
sit around for the rest of our lives and play
video games and smoke pot and try to figure

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.570
out what to do with ourselves, so I think
that's a misperception.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:34.470
I think, my perception, my perspective here
is coming from doing a lot of work with large

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:41.620
enterprises who are in the process of trying
to adopt and make real applications from a

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.790
lot of the theoretical research coming out
of academic research units and things like

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:45.790
computer vision.

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:50.240
So using computers and showing them pictures
of a puppy or pictures of a glass of wine

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.949
and without any associated metadata they can,
they can recognize that and they can accurately

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:57.730
label those tasks.

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:02.290
So in doing this work with enterprises, I've
seen that this is just a lot harder than one

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:03.290
would think.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:04.970
It's not like you can as in the Matrix, right?

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:08.952
If anybody's seen the Matrix where Keanu reeves
puts little chip in his brain and the next

00:06:08.952 --> 00:06:09.952
thing you know he can.

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:11.350
He's an expert karate master right?

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:15.460
We have this impression when we think about
augmented intelligence that the systems will

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.820
get so smart that we can put our little chips
in and then we're, we'll be fluent in German

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:24.320
after one day of work as opposed to passing
the Malcolm Gladwell 10,000 hours in order

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.009
to learn some new skill.

00:06:26.009 --> 00:06:33.080
But in practice it does take time and it often
takes an articulated, artfully articulated

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:38.110
collaboration between men and machine in order
to get going with systems.

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:44.000
When I'm working with large Wall Street banks
or insurance companies or media companies,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.259
often I'll meet with sort of an executive
layer and the impression that they have is

00:06:48.259 --> 00:06:55.690
that they can go from absolutely manual processes
to complete Netflix like automation in three

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:56.789
or four months.

00:06:56.789 --> 00:06:59.770
And they ask us to scope out projects like
this where they can just plug in a machine

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:05.810
and, you know, we're sort of ready to go,
and what I like about Stitch Fix, and I'll

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:09.180
use this as a metaphor to help them understand
what this might really look like…

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.550
Does everyone know what it is?

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:16.949
I'll explain it, so this is a, it's an e-commerce
shopping personal shopping site.

00:07:16.949 --> 00:07:22.919
So you go in and, say you're a woman and you're
sick and tired of shopping so you can sign

00:07:22.919 --> 00:07:27.960
up for an account and they ask you to fill
out a form that tells you some data about

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:28.960
yourself.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:32.960
So you know, your height, your weight, your
size, some of your taste in clothing.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.960
So you can go on Pinterest and pick out examples
of, of images, of clothing that you might

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:41.699
like and you send this off into the ether.

00:07:41.699 --> 00:07:46.569
And the next thing you know, a month later
they get a little box that has five items

00:07:46.569 --> 00:07:51.250
of clothing that the Stitch Fix assumes and
predicts might be, might be something that

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:52.250
you'd like.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.340
So this is partially algorithmic, right?

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:59.740
So the first past that Stitch Fix does is
to put this into some big algorithmic recommender

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:05.319
system where it parses the features that you've
input and then outputs recommended items,

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:12.181
but then they pass it to a set of personal
stylists who are just 1099 workers who go

00:08:12.181 --> 00:08:17.940
through and they curate a selection that that
will likely be of interest to the final and

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.319
end consumer of the service.

00:08:19.319 --> 00:08:24.770
So basically they've artfully combined human
intelligence to give feedback to those algorithms.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:30.820
And then all of the data oriented algorithmic
work, and I liked this as a metaphor or I

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:34.560
mean literal business model as well as a metaphor
for enterprises that are trying to get started

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:36.120
with these tools.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:41.300
Because if we take an example of say automating
a sales process in a large bank, there's a

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:46.170
lot of know how and knowledge and subject
matter expertise trapped within the heads

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:48.279
of the current employees.

00:08:48.279 --> 00:08:54.959
And the task here is to tease out some of
those insights and transform them into statistical

00:08:54.959 --> 00:08:58.589
patterns and systems so that there can be
this give and take and always with a feedback

00:08:58.589 --> 00:09:04.069
loop to eventually five years down the line,
maybe get to the point where the scales tip

00:09:04.069 --> 00:09:08.569
to the system's doing more of the work than
the people, but certainly not over overnight.

00:09:08.569 --> 00:09:13.879
So, the big misperception here I think is
the sort of inflated hyperbole around the

00:09:13.879 --> 00:09:17.579
fact that the machines are getting so smart,
they're gonna take over jobs tomorrow.

00:09:17.579 --> 00:09:25.879
Vassant, why don't you pick up on that, since
you’re in a domain it is, you know, quant

00:09:25.879 --> 00:09:27.129
hedge fund, if you will,

00:09:27.129 --> 00:09:28.129
Sure.

00:09:28.129 --> 00:09:35.439
So the way I sort of look at the financial
landscape is in terms of sort of high frequency

00:09:35.439 --> 00:09:40.869
trading where the machines out at the end
of the day just very short term decisions

00:09:40.869 --> 00:09:47.749
on one extreme and on the other hand you have
a long term decision making a la Warren Buffet

00:09:47.749 --> 00:09:55.360
style where you're looking at factors that
you know, the machine really has no basis

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:58.259
for picking up on and that's a very human

00:09:58.259 --> 00:09:59.910
The management team

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:00.910
Yes.

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.860
And it's just much more qualitative and there's
not enough data, right?

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:08.100
So it really boils down to does the machine
have enough data?

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.889
And then the middle you have sort of short
term trading where you might hold for days

00:10:11.889 --> 00:10:13.259
a weeks.

00:10:13.259 --> 00:10:17.179
So in the on the left hand side and the high
frequency side, there's lots of examples,

00:10:17.179 --> 00:10:22.279
lots of repeated instances, and you can learn
the machine has a basis for learning and that

00:10:22.279 --> 00:10:24.040
game was over a long time ago.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:26.759
Machines took that over from humans.

00:10:26.759 --> 00:10:31.629
On the right hand side you have machines that
really don't have a basis for making a decision.

00:10:31.629 --> 00:10:36.439
So that's an inherently human endeavor, not
that humans do particularly well at it.

00:10:36.439 --> 00:10:40.940
In fact, most humans actually underperformed
the market, you know, Warren Buffet and a

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:42.579
few others being notable exceptions.

00:10:42.579 --> 00:10:48.220
And in the middle you have sort of this intermediate
space where you have enough data and the machine

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:52.810
has a basis to learn and trade on its own.

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:58.160
My experience is that humans really aren't
capable of making too many decisions in a

00:10:58.160 --> 00:10:59.920
day, right?

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:05.379
If you actually have enough data, then models
of men tend to be better than man, right?

00:11:05.379 --> 00:11:10.259
That's what the research generally tends to
show, you know, human emotions kind of get

00:11:10.259 --> 00:11:12.459
in the way.

00:11:12.459 --> 00:11:17.309
And so, you know, when we talk about augmented
intelligence, you have to ask yourself who's

00:11:17.309 --> 00:11:24.350
augmenting who, you know, is the machine augmenting
humans, which has been sort of the traditional

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.339
decision support model for 50 years.

00:11:26.339 --> 00:11:30.619
And that makes a lot of sense because their
problems where you don't have enough data

00:11:30.619 --> 00:11:32.950
for the machine to actually make the, to be
making a decision.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.060
And so that makes a lot of sense.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:40.949
On the other hand, if it's machine augmenting
a, if it's man augmenting machine, then it,

00:11:40.949 --> 00:11:45.240
then I feel that it's sort of a matter of
time before the machine does better because

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:50.610
you're training it to do better and then you
should expect it to do better over time.

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:55.420
So sort of to put this all together, the way
I sort of look at the world is, you know,

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:59.060
if you can imagine sort of predictability
on the x axis and cost per error on the y

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:04.799
axis than problems that fall on the lower
right are very amenable to automated decision

00:12:04.799 --> 00:12:05.799
making.

00:12:05.799 --> 00:12:10.949
You know, you have high predictability and
low cost per error and problems to the left

00:12:10.949 --> 00:12:16.339
are difficult to automate because there's
low predictability relative to cost per error.

00:12:16.339 --> 00:12:20.889
Now, interestingly, driverless cars are highly
predictable until you might expect those to

00:12:20.889 --> 00:12:24.699
be automated, but at the moment the cost of
mistakes is very high.

00:12:24.699 --> 00:12:31.859
So they're also very high on the y axis, which
is why we're reluctant to cede control of

00:12:31.859 --> 00:12:38.370
our transportation yet to the machine because
we're just not sure of the sort of edge cases

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:39.559
or when stuff goes wrong.

00:12:39.559 --> 00:12:43.899
It could go badly wrong, so I suspect that
that will happen very gradually

00:12:43.899 --> 00:12:48.170
And isn't there another kind of aspect here
where there are categories of decisions.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:52.889
I mean so much of this technology, which we
used to call it data science, which is data

00:12:52.889 --> 00:12:54.610
plus machine learning algorithms.

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:58.189
We now call AI, right?

00:12:58.189 --> 00:13:04.430
Its principle use was increasing your odds
of making a sale, you know, predictive product

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:11.889
predictions, targeted advertising, that sort
of thing.

00:13:11.889 --> 00:13:15.980
And that's fine for decisions were better
on average is great.

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:19.859
To me, one of my favorite data sciences woman
named Claudia Perlich and her line on this,

00:13:19.859 --> 00:13:24.779
she works, used to work for a Watson and now
works for ad targeting firming.

00:13:24.779 --> 00:13:29.470
And her line is that, look, this is a great
time for experimentation in marketing because

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:31.049
what happens if my algorithm's wrong?

00:13:31.049 --> 00:13:33.500
It's, you know, it's somebody sees the wrong
ad.

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:37.350
It's not a false positive for breast cancer,
but we're moving into these categories that

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:38.720
things were better on you.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:46.009
You're affecting individuals' lives, medical
diagnosis, hiring decisions, and lending decisions

00:13:46.009 --> 00:13:50.980
where, you know, individual, these are high
stakes decisions for individual lives.

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:55.470
And that seems to me it's a different kind
of category, isn't it?

00:13:55.470 --> 00:14:01.350
Well, so for medical diagnosis, your trust
that falls sort of somewhere in the middle,

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:06.569
so let's say diabetes prediction than diagnosis
falls somewhere in the middle of the spectrum

00:14:06.569 --> 00:14:10.549
and machines do reasonably well, but they
still make lots of errors but it.

00:14:10.549 --> 00:14:14.480
So they still made this still significant
numbers of false positives and false negatives,

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:19.709
which can be particularly injurious if you
sort of missed something.

00:14:19.709 --> 00:14:24.809
And so for that reason, the way machines are
really used as sort of to make that first

00:14:24.809 --> 00:14:31.620
cart and categorize people into various levels
of risks that humans can then pursue in a

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:34.449
more structured kind of fashion.

00:14:34.449 --> 00:14:38.119
But it sort of comes down to that the cost
of errors there are still pretty high, so

00:14:38.119 --> 00:14:40.360
you're not going to cede control of that to
the machine.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:43.859
On the other hand, imagine that we now have
genomic data available, right?

00:14:43.859 --> 00:14:48.579
The trouble with healthcare is that most healthy
people have very few points of contact with

00:14:48.579 --> 00:14:49.749
the healthcare system.

00:14:49.749 --> 00:14:51.559
So there isn't enough data about them.

00:14:51.559 --> 00:14:52.559
Right.

00:14:52.559 --> 00:14:55.639
Whereas the sick people, there's lots of data
about them, but it's usually too late to do

00:14:55.639 --> 00:15:02.429
much about it, so you can imagine that in
the future as we get more data about people,

00:15:02.429 --> 00:15:06.470
your physician could very well be a machine
as well, or at least the machine group play

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:11.430
a much larger role in advising you on your
health.

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:13.239
Kathryn mentioned earlier, putting chips in
brains.

00:15:13.239 --> 00:15:19.559
Actually, this is an area of research that
Matthew's done a lot on and it talks about

00:15:19.559 --> 00:15:22.379
brain machine interfaces, some of primitive
ones we have now.

00:15:22.379 --> 00:15:27.069
And he explores, kind of the future of this
in terms of what you call the control problem.

00:15:27.069 --> 00:15:28.069
I believe so.

00:15:28.069 --> 00:15:29.069
Elaborate on that.

00:15:29.069 --> 00:15:30.879
So I'm a philosopher and bioethicists.

00:15:30.879 --> 00:15:35.560
So in the bioethics community, there's a talk
of human cognitive enhancement.

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:42.579
So one way to amplify our intelligence, so
is to sort of amplify biological intelligence

00:15:42.579 --> 00:15:46.359
to get us to become smarter biologically speaking.

00:15:46.359 --> 00:15:52.529
But there's another way which is to do some
sort of symbiosis where we begin to use computer

00:15:52.529 --> 00:15:55.799
parts and smartphones kinda like that.

00:15:55.799 --> 00:15:59.470
You might think that your smartphone, it's
kind of like an extended mind, an extension

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:00.790
of your mind.

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:05.589
But there's more now there's something called
brain machine interfaces where there's something

00:16:05.589 --> 00:16:07.139
called transcranial stimulation.

00:16:07.139 --> 00:16:09.699
So you sorta put something over your head.

00:16:09.699 --> 00:16:14.209
So companies are marketing this to athletes
so that they can sort of performed better.

00:16:14.209 --> 00:16:15.879
They can learn better, learn quicker.

00:16:15.879 --> 00:16:20.919
And there's actually evidence that it's not
quite like the matrix style of being able

00:16:20.919 --> 00:16:22.739
to learn a language right away.

00:16:22.739 --> 00:16:27.059
But it turns out that you can learn things
faster, you remember things more using even

00:16:27.059 --> 00:16:30.999
transcranial stimulation, which is actually
a very crude technology.

00:16:30.999 --> 00:16:35.449
And there's something else called deep brain
stimulation which is much more invasive.

00:16:35.449 --> 00:16:40.639
